{
  "raw": {
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/787": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Clinical Assistant Professor of Leadership and Policy Studies The University of Memphis College of Education, Department of Leadership, invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-track position as Clinical Assistant Professor of Leadership and Policy Studies. The position is based on the University’s main campus in Memphis, Tennessee. Regular on-campus presence is required for teaching, mentoring, collaboration, and other departmental responsibilities. This is not a remote position. The successful candidate will primarily work with students in the EdD in Leadership and Policy Studies program, with particular emphasis on supporting doctoral students through the dissertation-in-practice process. Responsibilities will include teaching and mentoring EdD students as they identify and refine problems of practice, develop researchable questions, design applied and action-oriented research studies, analyze data, and write and revise dissertation proposals and final dissertations. The successful candidate will be expected to serve on dissertation committees and provide sustained mentorship to doctoral students as they move through key program milestones. The position also includes teaching graduate courses in Educational Leadership and Policy Studies and mentoring master’s and doctoral students. Candidates should demonstrate expertise in applied research, action research, improvement-oriented inquiry, or related approaches appropriate for practitioner-focused doctoral education. The successful candidate will work with diverse student populations, collaborate with faculty and educational partners, contribute to program development and assessment, and provide service to the department, college, university, and profession. The start date for this position is August 16, 2027. Please note that the department is currently not offering sponsorship. The teaching load for this position is three (3) to four (4) classes per semester, depending on departmental needs. A doctoral degree in educational leadership, educational policy studies, or a closely related field, awarded no later than August 2027. • Demonstrated effectiveness or potential in teaching at the graduate level. • A minimum of two years of teaching in the K-12 setting. • Experience supporting doctoral students’ dissertation work. • Proven experience and effectiveness in online teaching environments. • Proven success in promoting and managing sustained online learning communities. • At least 3 years of professional experience in a relevant field (e.g., K-12, or higher education). • Identifiable success in advising or mentoring students. • Familiarity with the problem-of-practice dissertation model. • K-12 administrative experience. • Experience serving on dissertation committees. • A record of scholarly publications and/or presentations. • Eligibility for Associate or Full Member Graduate Faculty status. Teach a variety of online courses in leadership theory, organizational/systems thinking, and action research. Advise/coordinate doctoral students and serve on dissertation committees. Attend in-person college and departmental meetings and hold regular office hours."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/798": {
      "title": "Research Associate Professor, Quantum & Artificial Intelligence (QAI)",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The FedEx Institute of Technology is seeking a Research Associate Professor to lead its Quantum & Artificial Intelligence (QAI) research efforts. This is a renewable five-year position focused on translational research at the intersection of quantum computing, artificial intelligence, and emerging approaches toward artificial general intelligence (AGI), with an emphasis on advancing research into practical, deployable solutions. The successful candidate will provide scientific and programmatic leadership for this exciting new QAI discipline, establish a high-impact research agenda, build multidisciplinary collaborations, and develop externally funded research programs. The position is intended for a researcher who can bridge foundational advances with real-world applications and move promising ideas from research through prototyping, validation, and deployment. The FedEx Institute of Technology is seeking a Research Associate Professor to lead its Quantum & Artificial Intelligence (QAI) research efforts. This is a renewable five-year position focused on translational research at the intersection of quantum computing, artificial intelligence, and emerging approaches toward artificial general intelligence (AGI), with an emphasis on advancing research into practical, deployable solutions. The successful candidate will provide scientific and programmatic leadership for this exciting new QAI discipline, establish a high-impact research agenda, build multidisciplinary collaborations, and develop externally funded research programs. The position is intended for a researcher who can bridge foundational advances with real-world applications and move promising ideas from research through prototyping, validation, and deployment. Requires a PhD in Artificial Intelligence, Quantum Computing, Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a closely related field. At least five years of relevant research and/or professional experience in artificial intelligence, quantum computing, applied AI, or closely related areas. Evidence of research leadership, scholarly productivity, and the ability to develop collaborative research programs is expected. Candidates must demonstrate experience in the following areas: Research in quantum computing, quantum programming, quantum algorithms, quantum machine learning, or hybrid quantum-classical computing Advanced artificial intelligence and machine learning, including areas such as foundation models, reinforcement learning, natural language processing, computer vision, knowledge-based AI, agentic AI, or multi-agent systems Translational and applied AI, including the development, validation, and deployment of AI systems for real-world scientific, engineering, industrial, public-sector, or societal applications Research on generalizable intelligent systems and emerging approaches toward artificial general intelligence, including reasoning, planning, learning, adaptation, evaluation, robustness, and responsible AI Leadership of multidisciplinary research projects, laboratories, research centers, or technical teams, including mentoring of students and researchers Development of competitive grant proposals and externally funded research programs involving government, industry, foundations, or other sponsors Collaboration with external partners and experience translating research outcomes into prototypes, operational systems, technology transfer opportunities, or other forms of measurable impact Evidence of strong ability in research, mentoring, institutional service, and external engagement. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Provide scientific, technical, and strategic leadership for the university’s Quantum & Artificial Intelligence (QAI) research efforts Establish and execute a research agenda spanning quantum computing, artificial intelligence, applied AI, and emerging research toward artificial general intelligence (AGI) Lead translational research that converts algorithms, models, and scientific advances into prototypes, pilot systems, and deployable solutions Develop research in areas such as quantum algorithms, quantum machine learning, hybrid quantum-classical methods, advanced machine learning, foundation models, agentic and multi-agent systems, and intelligent decision systems Build and mentor multidisciplinary research teams involving faculty, research staff, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, undergraduate students, and technical collaborators Develop competitive proposals and secure external research funding from federal agencies, industry, foundations, and other sponsors Collaborate with academic, government, and industry partners to validate research in real-world settings and support technology transition, transfer, and broader impact Document and communicate research outcomes through peer-reviewed publications, technical reports, presentations, demonstrations, and other scholarly and professional activities"
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/773": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Undergraduate Engagement Librarian",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Assistant Professor, Undergraduate Engagement Librarian This is a full-time, nontenure-track faculty appointment and is exempt from wage and hour restrictions. Faculty are expected to work to the fulfillment of their duties and responsibilities as identified in the position description. The purpose of this position is to address the needs and promote the academic success of undergraduate students at the University, including supporting specific populations of undergraduate students, such as students from varied ethnic and cultural backgrounds, international students, and non-traditional students (ex. adult, transfer, veteran, degree completion, rural, First-Generation, disabled). The position helps provide direct user assistance at the Research Assistance Desk, virtual reference services, and through research appointments. Provides leadership in the Learning Engagement Team and collaboration with the Learning Experience Team within the Research and Instructional Services Department. As a member of the faculty, this position also serves on committees and task forces. Nontenure-track faculty are eligible for promotion to higher ranks by established criteria. ALA accredited master’s degree. Appropriate relevant library work experience. Provides and supports the services and programs of the Research and Instructional Services (RIS) Department and the University Libraries’ User Instruction Program. Takes responsibility for providing leadership and/or assistance with coordinating the University Libraries’ commitment to and support of the University’s undergraduate educational initiatives as well as programs orienting new students, including first-year students and transfer students. Develops specific programming addressing the needs and promoting the academic success of undergraduate students. Cooperates and collaborates with personnel in and outside of the Libraries (e.g. Center for Academic Success and Achievement, Office of First-Generation Student Success, and TRIO) as needed to accomplish the goals of the department and the University Libraries. Supervises the work of library staff in the Learning Engagement Team. Provides individual research consultations and group library instruction sessions. Provides direct user assistance at the Research Assistance Desk (RAD) and through virtual reference services on a scheduled basis. Participate in the information literacy faculty development program. Volunteers for departmental programming and University Libraries outreach events. Performs other duties as assigned. Participates in library-wide projects and activities as needed. Undertakes other tasks assigned by the Head of Research and Instructional Services and/or the Executive Director of University Libraries. Participates in faculty governance and provides input into library decision-making. Participates in faculty meetings and works with colleagues to implement the agreements reached through collective decision-making. Serves on committees and task forces in the libraries, on campus, throughout the state and the region, as well as on the national level. Maintains and documents a program of continual learning that promotes his/her oof the libraries’ organizational mission, which may include seeking grant funding to support research or experimentation in their field(s) of interest, seeking opportunities to publish research findings in professional publications and to make formal presentations at professional meetings. Participates in appropriate professional associations by attending conferences, making presentations, serving on committees, and/or holding elected office. Participates in formal and informal programs of continual learning that will enhance professional expertise and contribute to overall effectiveness as a faculty member. Maintains membership and active participation in appropriate professional organizations."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/791": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor and Director",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/10/2026 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Clinical Assistant Professor and Director The School of Communication Sciences and Disorders at the University of Memphis is seeking a skilled, innovative, and visionary speech-language pathologist with a strong clinical background who would like to work in a stimulating academic setting The Director would lead the continued development, implementation, and ongoing direction of the new online program for speech-language pathology assistants. This position is a twelve-month, non-tenure-track faculty position, beginning in January 2027. The continuation of this position beyond three years is contingent upon the SLP-A program's success. This is a great opportunity for a talented speech-language pathologist to join an energized and innovative faculty. The University of Memphis has nationally recognized professional graduate programs in Speech-Language Pathology and Audiology, a research-oriented PhD, and a bachelor’s degree in American Sign Language and Deaf Studies. The SLP-A program would be a new addition to the school’s offerings. The school houses the Memphis Speech and Hearing Center, which serves as both the experiential learning laboratory for students and as a comprehensive communication healthcare center to the Memphis metropolitan area. The Director is encouraged to establish a line of clinical service in his or her area of expertise. Professional activity is expected, and research opportunities are available. The School of Communication Sciences and Disorders works to create diverse and accessible work, educational, and research environments. The School welcomes a wide range of ideas, practices, and expertise across the breadth and depth of the human experience. Master’s degree or higher CCC-SLP and eligible for Tennessee state licensure Minimum of 4 years of clinical experience Minimum of 2 years of experience as a clinical educator Excellent communication, counseling/coaching, collaboration, organizational, and leadership skills Following areas are desired, but not required: Experience with telepractice/telesupervision Experience working in higher education Familiarity with online instructional technology and course design Knowledge of cultural responsiveness and accessible instructional practices Familiarity with SLP-A scope of practice and supervision requirements Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct Clinical Faculty Responsibilities: Work with faculty on curriculum and program outcomes/design Teach online coursework Service to School, University, and the profession Professional/leadership activity Maintain a line of clinical practice Clinical supervision Administrative Responsibilities: Oversee the design, development, and implementation of online SLP-A program Coordinate and oversee SLP-A fieldwork experiences Track program progress and outcomes, and report as necessary Establish policy and procedure for program implementation Recruit, advise and mentor students Ongoing program development and marketing Primarily responsible to perform professional services, provide instruction to students, and perform service in a variety of settings. Research activities may be included as part of the effort. Preparing students for the complexities and realities of the current professional environment; Staying current in their field of practice to ensure course content is consistent with the current professional setting; Organizing and supervising students in professional programs. This position is not eligible for tenure."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/735": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Oboe",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Rudi E. Scheidt School of Music at the University of Memphis invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Oboe, beginning Fall 2027. The successful candidate will be an artist-level performer and teacher with a proven record of recruiting and student success. The Rudi E. Scheidt School of Music at the University of Memphis invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Oboe, beginning Fall 2027. The successful candidate will be an artist-level performer and teacher with a proven record of recruiting and student success. Master's degree in oboe performance (or closely related field) required; doctorate preferred Demonstrated excellence in teaching applied oboe at the college level Extensive experience as a soloist, chamber musician, and/or orchestral musician Ability and desire to teach in an area outside of oboe Application : Candidates should go to: http://workforum.memphis.edu to submit an application. Required application materials: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Web links to 3 performances within the last 5 years (video preferred) Web links to 2 videos of representative teaching A list of at least three references with contact information Statement of teaching philosophy Review of applications will begin October 30, 2026, and may continue until a successful candidate is named. For further information contact: Dr. Robyn Jones, Associate Professor of Clarinet Chair - Assistant Professor of Oboe Search Committee (504) 250-4510 rljnes12@memphis.edu Teach applied oboe lessons, reed making, and pedagogy at the undergraduate levels. Coach chamber ensembles. Actively recruit, mentor, and retain oboe students; maintain an active and engaged studio. Collaborate and perform with the School of Music faculty. Teach coursework in an additional area (e.g., music theory, music history, etc.). Maintain regional., national, and international visibility through performing and teaching engagements. Serve on and/or chair doctoral committees."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/759": {
      "title": "Director of the IEAA and Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Memphis invites applications for Director of the Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology (IEAA), to be appointed at the rank of Assistant (tenure-track) or Associate (tenured) professor commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Memphis invites applications for Director of the Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology (IEAA), to be appointed at the rank of Assistant (tenure-track) or Associate (tenured) professor commensurate with qualifications and experience. This full-time position is funded by the Department of Art and Design and the Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology, a Tennessee Center of Excellence. The position reports directly to the Chair of the Department of Art and Design and will work collaboratively with other full-time faculty in both Art History and History, including the curator of the University of Memphis’s collection of Egyptian artifacts. Founded in 1984, the IEAA is a component of the College of Communication and Fine Arts, Department of Art and Design. The IEAA is dedicated to the study of the art and culture of ancient Egypt through teaching, research, exhibition, and community education. The IEAA also supports the fieldwork of faculty and students who participate in excavations and/or epigraphic projects in Egypt. Review of application materials will begin on January 1, 2027. A Ph.D. in Egyptology or a closely related field is required. As Director of the IEAA, the successful candidate should be familiar with new and emerging trends in research within Egyptology and have competence in Middle Egyptian. The successful candidate should possess experience teaching at the university level, mentoring and advising students, and applying for grants or other sources of external funding. 3 years of prior administrative or institutional fundraising experience is strongly preferred. Assistant (tenure-track) with less than 5 years of experience in the field Associate (tenured) professor if tenure has been achieved at another institution. Application Materials Applications will only be accepted through the University’s portal at https://workforum.memphis.edu. Candidates must upload PDFs of all the following: Cover Letter (2 pages maximum) Curriculum Vitae Statement describing teaching philosophy and vision for the IEAA, a research center at a public institution with a mandate for fundraising and community engagement (3 pages maximum; upload under “Teaching Philosophy”) Email addresses of three professional references Review of application materials will begin on January 1, 2027. Institution and Setting The Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology is a Tennessee Center of Excellence devoted to the study of the art and culture of ancient Egypt through teaching, research, exhibition, and community education. It is housed within the Department of Art and Design at the University of Memphis, in Memphis, Tennessee (USA). The University of Memphis is a research institution in the urban south situated in a city well known for a uniquely rich cultural legacy in the arts. The Department of Art and Design is housed within the College of Communication and Fine Arts along with the Departments of Architecture, Theatre and Dance, the Rudi E. Scheidt School of Music, the School of Communication and Media, the Art Museum of the University of Memphis (AMUM), and the Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology (IEAA). United by a shared commitment to discovery, creativity, and innovation, the College fosters an environment where students and faculty explore new ideas, challenge conventions, and shape the cultural landscape. The Department of Art and Design offers Bachelor of Arts, Bachelor of Fine Arts, Master of Arts, and Master of Fine Arts degrees in various fine arts, art history, and art education concentrations. The Department of Art & Design works closely with the Art Museum of The University of Memphis, which offers a Graduate Certificate Program in Museum Studies. With approximately 400 majors, 21 full-time faculty, and dedicated staff, the Department of Art and Design combines personal mentorship with the energy of an urban serving university. Further information can be found here: https://www.memphis.edu/artanddesign/ and https://www.memphis.edu/ccfa/ . Vision and Direction Develop a clear vision for the future direction of the IEAA, including Institute-sponsored events and activities, external institutional fundraising, and coordination with other Egyptological institutes and organizations at the local, national, and international levels. Organize exhibitions, lectures, and programs that engage audiences, fostering partnerships with academic institutions and community organizations, as well as Memphis’ many and various stakeholders. Cultivate proactive strategies for the expansion of the Institute’s programs. Oversee the operating budget for the Institute of Egyptian Art and Archaeology, including coordination with other Egyptology faculty and students on the allocation of funds. Coordinate with the Curator of the University of Memphis’ collection of Egyptian antiquities, including collections research, exhibition planning and management, website maintenance, and cooperation with lending institutions. Work with University Advancement to develop outside support for the Institute’s mission. Teaching and Advisement Teach a 1/2 course load, with courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Oversee the M.A. concentration in Egyptian Art and Archaeology; advising graduate students through the program. Serve on M.A. and Ph.D. committees in the Department of Art and Design and the Department of History. Professional Activity Maintain an active research and publication agenda within the candidate’s area(s) of expertise. Strengthen and maintain the national and international profile of the IEAA through coordination with outside scholars, attendance and participation at professional conferences, and development of public programs in collaboration with the Institute’s faculty in Egyptology."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/709": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/10/2026 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Sutter Laboratory in the Department of Biological Sciences at the University of Memphis is seeking a motivated postdoctoral fellow with strong experience in molecular and cell biology. You will join a NIH-funded multi-disciplinary team focused on the pharmacodynamics of aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) ligands and their system level effects on skin biology. Preference will be given to applicants with a strong publication record and experience in biochemistry, molecular biology, and immunology. Experience using complex analytical methods such as RNA-Seq, ChIP-Seq, and metabolomics, as well as experience in cell culture models, animal genetics and data analysis is beneficial. Strong oral and written communication skills are required. Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Competitive benefits package included. The Sutter Laboratory in the Department of Biological Sciences at the University of Memphis is seeking a motivated postdoctoral fellow with strong experience in molecular and cell biology. You will join a NIH-funded multi-disciplinary team focused on the pharmacodynamics of aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) ligands and their system level effects on skin biology. Preference will be given to applicants with a strong publication record and experience in biochemistry, molecular biology, and immunology. Experience using complex analytical methods such as RNA-Seq, ChIP-Seq, and metabolomics, as well as experience in cell culture models, animal genetics and data analysis is beneficial. Strong oral and written communication skills are required. Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Competitive benefits package included. PhD in a Life Science discipline. The PDF will conduct research in a laboratory setting that focuses on the pharmacodynamics of aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) ligands and their system level effects on skin biology. PDF is expected to perform complex experiments using analytical methods of biochemistry, molecular biology, and immunology. Examples of such methods include RNA-Seq, ChIP-Seq, metabolomics, and flow cytometry. The ability to perform these experiments in primary cell culture, organotypic culture and in vivo, in rodents, will be required. In addition, good laboratory record keeping and the willingness to participate in research compliance are expected. Strong oral and written communication skills, willingness to train others, and the ability to write and submit scientific manuscripts are required."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/734": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Finance, Insurance, & Real Estate",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Department of Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate in the Fogelman College of Business & Economics at the University of Memphis is seeking qualified applicants for a tenure track Assistant Professor of Finance position, with teaching duties beginning as soon as possible. This is a nine-month, tenure-track appointment. Hiring is contingent on final budget approval. The Assistant Professor will teach, conduct research, and participate in service leadership primarily at the Memphis Campus, including online though teaching at off-campus sites may be required. The Department of Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate in the Fogelman College of Business & Economics at the University of Memphis is seeking qualified applicants fo a tenure track Professor of Finance position, with teaching duties beginning as soon as possible. This is a nine-month, tenure-track appointment. Hiring is contingent on final budget approval. The Assistant Professor will teach, conduct research, and participate in service leadership primarily at the Memphis Campus, including online though teaching at off-campus sites may be required. The Fogelman College of Business & Economics has become a leading business school in the Southeast. The academic programs of the College are fully accredited by the AACSB (Association to Advance Collegiate Schools of Business). The College’s full-time faculty of 96 serves approximately 3,165 students, with nearly 2,550 undergraduates, 550 master’s students, and 65 doctoral students. The Department of Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate includes 13 full-time faculty members in addition to this position. Additional information about the University, College, and Department is available at the University’s official website: http://www.memphis.edu . A Ph.D. or ABD in finance is required. Research and professional engagement activities sufficient to qualify as Scholarly Academic under existing AACSB business accreditation guidelines are strongly desired. Applications Applications should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, unofficial transcript of highest degree earned, the names and contact information of three professional references, and be made at http://workforum.memphis.edu . Screening of applications begins immediately and may continue until the position is filled. The search committee chair is Dr. Konstantin Sokolov, Department of Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate, (901) 678-5930, ksokolov@memphis.edu. The University of Memphis, is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. All qualified applicants are urged to apply for this position. Appointment will be based on qualifications as they relate to the position requirements without regard to race, color, national origin, religion, sex, age, disability, or veteran status. Job responsibilities include effectively teaching graduate and undergraduate finance courses, research productivity, grant proposals, significant service leadership on important departmental operations related to online programs and the Cook Analytics & Trading Lab. Instructional duties include effective instruction in undergraduate finance courses that are also cross listed at the Master’s level; evaluating student learning outcomes; participating in related department activities; and complying with all Department, College, and University regulations. The Assistant Professor position will also be responsible for teaching courses that lead to financial certifications and licensing (CFP/CFA) and maintaining FIR’s undergraduate and MSBA program recognition by these institutions. Job duties include: 1. Teaching four (4) finance courses for the academic year (two course per semester) and ensuring high rates of success in student learning outcomes. 2. Maintenance of AACSB scholarly academic status through research and targeting top tier journal publications included in the UTD, FT50, and departmental A+ lists. 3. Assisting with Cook Analytics & Trading Lab operations including lab scheduling, dataset maintenance, tech support liaison for hardware and software issues as well as online website and eLearn support to improve the department’s worldwide web visibility through department resources. 4. Assisting with recruitment of students and student leaders for clubs and teams along with supporting students in finance competitions, the CFP Board educational program, inter-university student competitions, Finance Club, and other university activities. Maintaining contact with the CFA Society, the FPA Society along with maintaining AACSB scholarly academic credentials. 5. Service on Department and College Committees."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/692": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Epidemiology",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United States Hire a full-time, 9-month tenure-track Assistant Professor in Epidemiology in the Division of Epidemiology, Biostatistics, and Environmental Health. The School of Public Health (SPH) at the University of Memphis (UofM) is seeking to hire a full-time, 9-month tenure-track Assistant Professor in Epidemiology to join our interdisciplinary, collaborative team of faculty. The position is in the Division of Epidemiology, Biostatistics, and Environmental Health. Position Qualifications Candidates should have an earned doctoral degree in Epidemiology or a closely related field and experience commensurate with the academic rank of assistant professor. Candidates with a background and interest in research and teaching in chronic diseases, aging, allergic diseases, cardiovascular diseases, mental health, rural health, health informatics and artificial intelligence, or genetics and epigenetics, and have strong potential of securing extramural research funds, are especially encouraged to apply. Duties and responsibilities Teaching four (4) 3-credit hour courses (2 each semester) in areas of Epidemiology and related fields. Advising and mentoring graduate students. Seeking extramural grants to support investigator-led research activity in a collaborative environment with focus in one or more of the following areas: chronic diseases, aging, allergic diseases, cardiovascular diseases, mental health, rural health, health informatics and artificial intelligence, or genetics and epigenetics. Engaging in interdisciplinary work and community-based research, collaborating with faculty from public health, computer science, engineering, and related fields to advance SPH initiatives. Developing and maintaining an active research program and publication agenda with peer-reviewed publications and extramural funding. Serving on committees to further develop, support, and enhance the Division and the School of Public Health."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/690": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor, Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Herff College of Engineering at The University of Memphis invites applications for a dynamic, highly collaborative Assistant Research Professor (ARP). This specialized, non-tenure-track faculty position serves as the vital technical and intellectual bridge across three core departments: Biomedical Engineering (BME), Civil Engineering (CIVL), and Mechanical Engineering (MECH). The successful candidate will manage a Shared Materials Lab environment and establish a central \"Fabrication-to-Function\" nexus. The primary focus of this role is to accelerate interdisciplinary research, generate critical preliminary data for federal grants, and pioneer the development of sustainable, bio-inspired materials alongside advanced additive manufacturing techniques. Position Overview The Herff College of Engineering at The University of Memphis invites applications for a dynamic, highly collaborative Assistant Research Professor (ARP) . This specialized, non-tenure-track faculty position serves as the vital technical and intellectual bridge across three core departments: Biomedical Engineering (BME), Civil Engineering (CIVL), and Mechanical Engineering (MECH). The successful candidate will manage a Shared Materials Lab environment and establish a central \"Fabrication-to-Function\" nexus . The primary focus of this role is to accelerate interdisciplinary research, generate critical preliminary data for federal grants, and pioneer the development of sustainable, bio-inspired materials alongside advanced additive manufacturing techniques. Core Strategic Themes The ARP will center their research, fabrication efforts, and grant writing around four core thematic pillars: Bio-regenerative scaffolds for tissue engineering. Sustainable \"Green\" composites for civil infrastructure applications. Multi-scale fabrication of highly functional materials. Multi-scale mechanical characterization of hybrid materials. Key Collaborations The ARP will work with a robust, pre-established cohort of materials science and engineering experts, including but not limited to: Biomedical Engineering: Dr. Amber Jennings (Lead Mentor, Infection/Biomaterials), Dr. Joel Bumgardner (Dental Regeneration), Dr. Gary Bowlin (Tissue Templates/Electrospinning), and Dr. Hayden Carlton (Thermal Therapy). Civil Engineering: Dr. Farhad Jazei (Co-Lead, Sustainable Infrastructure) and Dr. Yuankai Huang (Sensors & Functionalized Materials). Mechanical Engineering: Dr. Yuan Gao (Technical Lead, Microfluidics/MPS), Dr. Gladius Lewis (Bone Cement/Stents), and Dr. Ranganathan Gopalakrishnan (Aerosol Delivery & Processes). External Synergy: Potential to facilitate broader inter-college collaborations with faculty in Physics, Chemistry, and Biology. Candidates must submit their application through https://workforum.memphis.edu . Click on the full-time faculty menu to find the Biomedical Engineering posting. Applications must include a cover letter, a comprehensive curriculum vitae, the full names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) of three professional references, and a statement on research philosophy. Screening of applications will begin in August 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Minimum Qualifications Education: Ph.D. in Materials Science, Biomedical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a closely related engineering discipline. Experience: Proven track record in advanced additive manufacturing, multi-scale material fabrication, and mechanical characterization protocols. Skills: Demonstrated ability to write and submit competitive federal grant proposals (NIH, NSF, USDA). Strong cross-disciplinary communication skills, with the agility to interface between micro-biological and macro-structural application spaces. Experience managing shared laboratory spaces and mentoring student researchers. 1. Proposal Development & Financial Sustainability High-Frequency Grant Writing: Serve as a primary driver for an aggressive external proposal pipeline, aiming for at least five external submissions per year targeting the NSF, NIH, and USDA. Interdisciplinary Team Building: Co-author multi-departmental, multi-investigator grant proposals (e.g., NSF GCR, NIH P01) to bridge BME, CIVL, and MECH expertise. Targeted Funding Goals: Drive the strategic target of securing over $2.5M in requested funds over 24 months, with an expectation to secure at least one major award exceeding $300,000 by the end of Year 2. Transition to Independence: Actively manage the position's funding transition from 100% institutional support to 50% external grant funding by Month 12, and 100% by Month 24. 2. Research, Fabrication & Lab Management Rapid Preliminary Data Generation: Pivot efficiently between the microscopic demands of drug delivery systems/biomaterials and the macroscopic requirements of civil infrastructure to provide high-quality preliminary data for faculty submissions. Shared Materials Lab Supervision: Manage the daily operations of the Shared Materials Lab environment. Interdisciplinary Cross-Pollination: Convene and facilitate weekly \"sandpit\" meetings for BME, CIVL, and MECH students and faculty to brainstorm fabrication-application fits and streamline characterization protocols. Contingency & Industry Liaison: If federal timelines experience delays, pivot strategically toward generating industry-sponsored research contracts by providing fee-for-service medical device testing and validation for private sector partners. 3. Scholarly Output & Mentorship High-Impact Publications: Disseminate research by maintaining a steady publication record of at least three peer-reviewed articles per year in premier journals (e.g., Biomaterials , Acta Materialia , Advanced Functional Materials ). Conference Presentations: Present pioneering fabrication methodologies and findings at prestigious national and international venues, such as the Society for Biomaterials, Orthopaedic Research Society (ORS), and Materials Research Society (MRS). Student Mentorship: Serve as a technical mentor for both undergraduate and graduate student-led research initiatives across diverse engineering backgrounds."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/633": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Health Economics",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Assistant Professor in Health Economics, School of Public Health Full-Time Non-Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Health Economics to join our interdisciplinary and collaborative faculty team. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to research, teaching, and service activities; develop and sustain externally funded research programs; collaborate across disciplines; and support the school’s mission of advancing population health through innovative, evidence-based solutions. Candidates should possess a PhD in Economics, Health Economics, Health Informatics, Health Care Policy, Public Health, Health Services Research, Healthcare Administration, or a closely related field, with experience commensurate with the rank of Assistant Professor. Preferred qualifications include expertise in one or more of the following areas: health economics, health informatics and artificial intelligence, aging and dementia, rural health, and health policy. The successful candidate should demonstrate the potential to develop a sustained research agenda, secure external funding, publish in peer-reviewed journals, and contribute to the educational mission of the school through teaching, advising, and mentorship. Duties and Responsibilities • Develop and lead research programs in health economics, health informatics and artificial intelligence, aging and dementia, rural health, and health policy. • Design, conduct, and evaluate research studies that inform the effective use of healthcare information, resources, and public health interventions. • Perform economic evaluations, cost-benefit analyses, cost-effectiveness analyses, and health economic modeling for healthcare programs, public health initiatives, and policy decisions. • Develop and maintain an active research and publication agenda and seek external funding from federal, state, foundation, and industry sources. • Analyze, prepare, and disseminate research reports, peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and other scholarly products; • Teach one to two courses annually in areas such as health economics, Health informatics, healthcare financing, health program evaluation, health technology assessment, health policy, and related subjects. • Provide educational programs, lectures, workshops, and training activities during the academic year and the summer semester."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/624": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Clinical Assistant Professor, Audiology This 12-month, non-tenure track clinical faculty position is ideal for a strong, innovative audiologist whose focus area is primarily adults and who will provide clinical education in an academic setting. The School of Communication Sciences and Disorders at the University of Memphis (www.memphis.edu/csd) is one of the leading academic and clinical programs in the country. This 12-month non-tenure track position is ideal for a strong, innovative clinician whose focus area is primarily adults and who will provide clinical education in an academic setting. The clinical program offers the full scope of audiology services to our patients including diagnostic assessments across the lifespan, hearing aid services, cochlear implant services, bone conduction device services, electrophysiology assessments, vestibular assessments, auditory rehabilitation, and auditory processing disorder testing and treatment. Applicants are expected to collaborate on research and service with clinical and academic faculty. This candidate is expected to be involved in the maintenance and growth of the audiology program. Applicants with experience in adult hearing evaluations and adult hearing aid services are strongly encouraged to apply. Experience with cochlear implant services is preferred, though not required. This position requires an AuD or PhD. The applicant must have ASHA certification (CCC-A) and be eligible for TN state licensure. We are excited to welcome motivated, team-oriented candidates that will complement and expand our existing programmatic strengths. As a clinical faculty member, there is an expectation to provide service to the School, including participating in committees and meetings. About Us The Memphis Speech and Hearing Center (MSHC) stands as one of the nation's oldest and most distinguished communication healthcare facilities, serving the Memphis community since 1947. As an integral part of the University of Memphis since 1967, clinical excellence is combined with academic innovation through the School of Communication Sciences and Disorders. Our dynamic team of licensed clinicians and graduate trainees serves thousands of patients annually, providing comprehensive diagnostic and treatment services for speech, language, cognitive, and hearing and balance disorders across all age groups. The Center is located at 4055 North Park Loop, on the Park Avenue campus of the University of Memphis. It features state-of-the-art research laboratories alongside our clinical facilities, establishing MSHC as one of the premier speech and hearing facilities in the nation. Our mission is threefold: Deliver high-quality, innovative services in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of communication disorders to Memphis area residents of all ages Provide educational and training opportunities for clients and their families Offer exceptional clinical education opportunities for future communication disorders professionals Minimum Position Requirements: AuD or PhD in audiology Tennessee state licensure in audiology, or eligibility Certificate of Clinical Competence (CCC-A) from the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association (ASHA) At least 2 years of postgraduate experience Completed at least .2 ASHA continuing education units (CEUs) (2 hours) in clinical education, and .1 CEU (1 hour) in ethics obtained within the past credentialing period. If this is not available, SCSD will assist with an independent study for ASHA CE credit Evidence of potential ability in teaching, service to the institution, service to the profession, and contribution to student development and success. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Applicants must be authorized to work in the U.S. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. Applicants with experience in adult hearing evaluations and adult hearing aid services are strongly encouraged to apply. Experience with cochlear implant services is preferred, though not required. Primarily responsibile to provide clinical instruction to graduate students, provide clinical service in the Memphis Speech and Hearing Center, the learning laboratory of the School of CSD, and provide service to the School and professions. Generally are not expected to conduct research, however, research activities may be included as part of the effort. Preparing students for the complexities and realities of the current professional environment; Staying current in their field of practice to ensure course/clinical practice content is consistent with the current professional practice; Organizing and supervising students in professional programs. This position is not eligible for tenure."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/411": {
      "title": "Clinical School Counseling Associate Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Assoc Professor POSITION The College of Education and the Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research seeks a full-time, 9-month, non-tenure-track faculty member for the School Counseling program beginning August 1, 2026. The School Counseling program is CACREP and CAEP accredited and emphasizes evidence-based practice promoting college and career readiness, datadriven programming, and interventions for students' academic achievement and social/emotional well-being. The position requires a balance of teaching, service, and clinical responsibilities. Teaching includes instructing graduate courses, advising/mentoring students, and serving on doctoral committees. Service includes participating in national professional organizations, university, and college committees, and serving the local community. Clinical responsibilities include providing practicum and internship supervision and assisting with field placements. The employed candidate will primarily teach courses within the School Counseling program but may also teach courses in the department’s other CACREP accredited counseling programs. Our CACREP programs are accredited for in-person delivery; therefore, this is not a remote position. The code number is 10413. The Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research and the College of Education are not currently offering any sponsorships for this position. SETTING The University of Memphis, an independently governed institution, is recognized by the Carnegie Foundation as one of two public research universities and the only community engaged public university in Tennessee. Boasting 12 colleges and professional schools, the University serves a diverse population of 22,000 students; 5,000 of whom are enrolled in graduate programs. Strongly committed to its urban mission and outreach programs, the University of Memphis recognizes the important role played by faculty and administrators in fulfilling a comprehensive mission of research, education, and community outreach. The Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research (CEPR) offers five graduate certificates, five master's degrees, and three doctoral degrees in its three principal areas. The department’s programs are accredited by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (see www.memphis.edu/cepr) and the American Psychological Association. The College of Education, with a faculty of over 75 full-time faculty positions, accredited by APA, CACREP, and CAEP is a leader in educator preparation. The College is dedicated to fairness and respect for the dignity of each person and seeks to prepare students for careers as administrators, counselors, educators, leaders, practitioners, researchers, scholars, and teachers. For additional information please see the websites of the university (http://www.memphis.edu), the college (http://www.memphis.edu/coe/), and the department (http:www.memphis.edu/cepr). SALARY & BEGINNING DATE Salary will be competitive and commensurate with qualifications. The University offers an excellent benefits package including healthcare and retirement. The position (code #10413) is a nine-month appointment with the possibility of summer teaching. The anticipated date of appointment is August 1, 2026. APPLICATION Review of applications will begin on June 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications are to be submitted electronically at WorkforUM2 Applicants are required to upload (1) a letter of application, (2) a statement of school counseling and clinical supervision experience, (3) a statement of teaching experience and philosophy, (4) a curriculum vitae, and (5) names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three references. Applicants will receive a confirmation number when all application materials are submitted. Address inquiries and nominations to Dr. Michelle Welch Brasfield (rmwelch@memphis.edu). Required • Doctoral degree in Counselor Education and Supervisor from a CACREP accredited program or meet one of the alternative qualifications according to CACREP standards • Licensed or license-eligible as a PK-12 school counselor in Tennessee • Minimum of five years of employment as a school counselor • Graduate level teaching experience • Strong knowledge of the ASCA National Model • Experience in providing clinical supervision Desired • Experience teaching classes either in-person, online, and/or hybridized environments in a CACREP accredited or CACREP aligned program • Knowledge and experience of continuous program evaluation and experience with CACREP and/or NCATE/CAEP • Evidence of federally funded grant awards or participation in federally funded projects • Teaching/clinical experience in one of the following areas: youth with disabilities, behavioral disorders, suicidal ideation, or those who have experienced trauma Evidence of potential ability in instruction, service, and research. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Applicants must be authorized to work in the U.S. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. Provide high-quality instruction, pursue excellence in research/scholarship/creative activity, and contribute to meaningful service to the university. Contribute to the missions of teaching, research / scholarship / creative activity, and public service."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/576": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor, Mathematics",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Visiting Assistant Professor, Mathematics. One-year non-tenure track position beginning August 2026. Teaching, research, and student mentoring responsibilities. The Department of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Memphis invites applications for a Visiting Assistant Professor position. This is a non-tenure track position for a fixed one-year period, with a target start date in August 2026 running through June 2027. Duties include carrying out a collaborative or independent research program in one of the Department's research areas (evidenced by publishing articles, attending conferences, and giving research talks, teaching a 2/1 course load for the academic year (9-11 credit hours per year), and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. Health insurance benefits are included. The position is contingent upon available funding and is for a one-year term, with the possibility of renewal. All strong candidates in one of the Department's research areas are encouraged to apply, particularly those with potential for collaboration with one or more of our faculty members. Applicants must be authorized to work in the U.S. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. For questions regarding the position, please contact: Dr. Ben McCarty Ben.McCarty@memphis.edu Minimum Qualifications: • Ph.D. in Mathematics completed before the position start date. • Adequate English skills sufficient to allow teaching mathematics at all levels of instruction. Preferred Qualifications: • The potential research and ability to function in one of the mathematics research areas of the Department will receive primary considerations. • Ability to bridge multiple research areas. • Evidence of the ability to teach effectively in a classroom setting. Research Areas: • Applied Mathematics (including Partial Differential Equations and Control Theory) • Combinatorics (including Graph Theory and Algebraic and Additive Combinatorics) • Dynamical Systems • Functional Analysis • Topology (including Low Dimensional Topology and Knot Theory) • Carry out a collaborative or independent research program in one of the Department's research areas. • Publish articles. • Attend conferences. • Give research talks. • Teach a 2/1 course load for the academic year (9-11 credit hours per year). • Mentor undergraduate and graduate students."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/476": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor, Implementation Science and Urban Health",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Use vacant position 011277 from Banner. Not sure of Oracle number. Seeking to hire Associate/Full Professor full-time, 9-month tenure-track Associate/Full Professor in Implementation Science and Urban Health to join our interdisciplinary, collaborative team of faculty The School of Public Health (SPH) at the University of Memphis (UofM) is seeking to hire a full-time, 9-month tenure-track Associate/Full Professor in Implementation Science and Urban Health to join our interdisciplinary, collaborative team of faculty. The successful candidate will be expected to teach both graduate and undergraduate students, enhance existing academic programs, develop an active research program, secure funding in promising areas of research, and foster collaborations within the school, university, and with external partners. Candidates should have a doctoral degree in Public Health, Social and Behavioral Sciences, Implementation Science, Urban Health, or a closely related field and experience commensurate with the academic rank of Associate/Full Professor. The successful candidate is expected to have demonstrated teaching ability, established research trajectory, and will be required to advise, mentor, and guide students to prepare them for the workforce. This is an in-person position and will be based in Memphis, TN, with an anticipated start date of Summer or Fall 2026. Duties and Responsibilities: The successful candidate will advance implementation science and urban health research with a focus on translating evidence-based interventions into sustainable, real-world practice in diverse and underserved urban populations. Responsibilities include to: Develop and sustain a funded research program in implementation science and urban health, including areas such as chronic disease prevention, mental health, structural and social determinants of health, and community-based interventions. Apply and advance implementation science and dissemination frameworks, including pragmatic trials, and strategies to improve adoption, implementation, and sustainability of evidence-based practices. Advance community-engaged and participatory research, working collaboratively with public health agencies and community-based organizations, particularly within urban contexts. Collaborate with faculty from public health, computer science, engineering, urban affairs, and related fields to advance SPH initiatives and interdisciplinary research. Teach four (4) 3-credit hour courses (2 each semester) in areas such as implementation science, social and behavioral sciences, urban health, health disparities, and related fields. Maintain an active research and publication agenda with peer-reviewed publications and extramural funding. Mentor graduate and undergraduate students, including doctoral students, and contribute to workforce development in implementation science and urban health. Contribute to curriculum development and program innovation, particularly in strengthening training in implementation science, dissemination research, and applied urban health. Demonstrate excellent oral and written communication skills and the ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary and community settings."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/410": {
      "title": "Associate Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Associate/Full Professor in Epidemiology, School of Public Health, University of Memphis The School of Public Health (SPH) at the University of Memphis (UofM) is seeking to hire a full-time, 9-month tenured Associate/Full Professor in Epidemiology to join our interdisciplinary, collaborative team of faculty. The successful candidate will be expected to teach both graduate and undergraduate students, enhance existing educational programs, develop an active research program, secure funding in promising areas of research, and foster collaborations within the school, university, and with external partners. Candidates should have an earned doctoral degree in Epidemiology or in a closely related field and experience commensurate with the academic rank of Associate/Full Professor. The successful candidate is expected to have demonstrated teaching ability, established research trajectory, and will be required to advise, mentor, and guide students to prepare them for the workforce. Demonstrated ability to sustain a funded research program in some of the following areas: epidemiology of chronic diseases, cardiovascular diseases, allergic diseases, mental health, genetics and epigenetics, and aging. Engage in interdisciplinary work, collaborating with faculty from public health, computer science, engineering, and related fields to advance SPH initiatives. Teach four (4) 3-credit hour courses (2 each semester) in areas of Epidemiology and related fields. Maintain an active research and publication agenda with peer-reviewed publications and extramural funding. Exhibit a passion for student mentoring and a commitment to participating in program development; and Have excellent oral and written communication skills and the ability to work effectively with others."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/816": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Clinical Assistant Professor of Leadership Studies The Department of Leadership seeks to fill a full-time, 9-month Clinical Assistant Professor position with responsibilities in Leadership Studies, beginning August 16, 2027. The Leadership Studies program will be a new undergraduate program with an emphasis on providing students with a meaningful, experiential education in the field of Leadership. The degree will develop students' capacity to lead with integrity, think critically, and collaborate effectively across diverse contexts. Through interdisciplinary learning and applied practice, the program will prepare graduates to influence positive change, strengthen communities, and excel as ethical, adaptative leaders in a complex world. The program’s emphasis is new to the College of Education, and the individual in this position will play a critical role in developing the curriculum, identifying experiential learning placements, and creating a recruitment plan. The successful candidate will contribute to the Department of Leadership’s broader instructional mission through teaching courses related to leadership development, group and team development, collaboration, and community-based leadership. Responsibilities may include teaching courses that examine how individuals and groups develop leadership capacity, function effectively as teams, navigate group dynamics, build collaborative relationships, and work collectively to address systemic needs in organizations and communities. The position provides an opportunity for an innovative educator to contribute to the development our growing catalogue of undergraduate offerings while connecting experiential education with contemporary approaches to leadership, group development, community engagement, and collaborative practice. Doctoral degree in an area that focuses on and includes a broad understanding of leadership (e.g., PhD/EdD in Educational Leadership, Higher Education Administration, Non-Profit Leadership, Organization Leadership, etc.) Demonstrated experience with teaching, preferably at the collegiate level; leadership development, group development, and/or collaborative learning. Strong interest in program building, including curriculum and partnership development. Understanding of theories of leadership development and community building Preferred: • Ability to network with other University colleges and departments, community advocates, and local leaders • Experience with both formal and non-formal/adult learning settings • Experience with grant writing and securing external project funding The successful candidate will contribute to the Department of Leadership’s broader instructional mission through teaching courses related to leadership development, group and team development, collaboration, and community-based leadership. Responsibilities may include teaching courses that examine how individuals and groups develop leadership capacity, function effectively as teams, navigate group dynamics, build collaborative relationships, and work collectively to address systemic needs in organizations and communities. The position provides an opportunity for an innovative educator to contribute to the development our growing catalogue of undergraduate offerings while connecting experiential education with contemporary approaches to leadership, group development, community engagement, and collaborative practice."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/821": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, School of Accountancy",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Crews School of Accountancy invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor, effective the spring semester of 2027 (preferred) or fall semester of 2027. Responsibilities include effectively teaching undergraduate and graduate courses across our diverse programs, conducting a stream of quality research, and fulfilling professional and university service responsibilities. The Crews School of Accountancy invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor, effective the spring semester of 2027 (preferred) or fall semester of 2027. Responsibilities include effectively teaching undergraduate and graduate courses across our diverse programs, conducting a stream of quality research, and fulfilling professional and university service responsibilities. Screening of applications begins November 2nd, 2026, and may continue until the position is filled. If you have questions, you are welcome to contact: As the largest academic unit in the Fogelman College of Business and Economics, the Crews School of Accountancy holds its own AACSB International accreditation in addition to the College's accreditation, distinguishing us among elite business programs worldwide. Our academically diverse, research-oriented group of 14 full-time faculty members offers degree programs at the bachelor's, master's, and doctoral levels. Our Master of Science in Accounting Analytics program features STEM-designated concentrations in Information Systems and Controls, Business Analysis and Reporting, and Tax Compliance and Planning. Our doctoral program provides both behavioral and archival research pathways, preparing scholars for successful academic careers. The School is committed to innovation in accounting education, including the integration of artificial intelligence and emerging technologies into our curriculum. We seek faculty who share our vision for preparing students to excel in a rapidly evolving profession while maintaining the highest standards of analytical rigor and professional excellence. For more information about the Crews School, visit https://www.memphis.edu/accountancy/ . A Ph.D. in accounting or a related business field from an AACSB-accredited institution is required (advanced ABDs may be considered if degree completion is imminent). Scholarly expertise in behavioral/experimental research and teaching excellence in accounting information systems (AIS) and data analytics is desired. Effective college teaching experience and evidence of strong research potential are both required for the position. This is an academic-year appointment and offers competitive compensation, summer research support, and an attractive benefits program, depending on qualifications. Applications Application packages should include a cover letter outlining qualifications and interests, a detailed curriculum vitae, a research sample, unofficial transcripts, and contact information for three professional references, and be submitted online at https://workforum.memphis.edu/ . We urge all qualified applicants to apply for this position. Appointment will be based on qualifications as they relate to position requirements. Screening of applications begins November 2nd, 2026, and may continue until the position is filled. If you have questions, you are welcome to contact: Dr. Hannah Smith Antinozzi, Search Committee Chair hsmith3@memphis.edu A faculty member who can teach accounting information systems (AIS) and data analytics courses is preferred, but all relevant areas may be considered. The Crews School values a multidisciplinary orientation, and the successful candidate will collaborate with faculty across the Fogelman College, including on research projects with accounting faculty and doctoral students in mutually relevant areas. Applicants from closely related disciplines whose research and teaching inform the study of accounting are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/820": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor, School of Accountancy",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Crews School of Accountancy at the University of Memphis invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor of Accounting, effective Spring 2027. This is a non-tenure-track, academic-year (9-month), teaching-focused appointment. The Crews School of Accountancy at the University of Memphis invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor of Accounting, effective Spring 2027. This is a non-tenure-track, academic-year (9-month), teaching-focused appointment. The successful candidate will provide high-quality instruction at the undergraduate and graduate levels in both in-person and online formats. Teaching strength in one or more of the following areas is preferred: taxation, financial accounting, governmental accounting, managerial accounting, business law, or a related area. The Crews School of Accountancy is the largest academic unit in the Fogelman College of Business and Economics and holds separate AACSB International accounting accreditation in addition to the College’s business accreditation. The School offers bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral programs in accounting and is committed to providing students with relevant and rigorous preparation for the accounting profession. For more information about the Crews School of Accountancy, visit https://www.memphis.edu/accountancy/ . A master’s degree in accounting, taxation, or a closely related business field from an AACSB-accredited institution. At least 18 graduate credit hours of accounting coursework. Demonstrated commitment to teaching excellence. Ability to teach effectively in in-person and/or online formats. Ability to work collaboratively with students and faculty. Preferred Qualifications Teaching strength, academic preparation, or professional expertise in one or more of the following areas: taxation, financial accounting, governmental accounting, managerial accounting, business law, or a related area. A Master of Taxation (MTax), Master of Science in Taxation (MST), or comparable graduate degree in taxation. A PhD or DBA in accounting or a closely related field, or ABD status with anticipated degree completion. Evidence of teaching effectiveness. Relevant professional accounting experience and/or professional certifications. Ability to collaborate with faculty and doctoral students on research or other scholarly activities. This academic-year appointment offers competitive compensation and benefits commensurate with qualifications and experience. Screening of applications will begin immediately and may continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding the position may be directed to: Dr. Kate Sorensen Search Committee Chair kbsrnsen@memphis.edu Appointment will be based on qualifications as they relate to the requirements and needs of the position. Application Materials Applicants should submit the following materials online: Cover letter outlining qualifications and interests; Detailed curriculum vitae; Statement of teaching philosophy; Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as a summary of recent teaching evaluations; Unofficial transcripts; and Contact information for three professional references. Provide high-quality instruction in undergraduate and graduate accounting courses in both in-person and online formats. Contribute to the School’s instructional needs in one or more areas, including taxation, financial accounting, governmental accounting, managerial accounting, business law, or a related area. Develop and maintain effective course materials. Support student learning, professional development, and academic success. Provide service to the School, College, and University. Support student organizations, internship programs, and other student professional-development activities. Collaborate with faculty and, where appropriate, doctoral students on academic and scholarly activities."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/828": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/06/2027 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Chemistry Department at The University of Memphis invites applications for Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty position to start August 1, 2027. The successful candidate will be expected to: establish a vigorous, innovative, externally funded research program; recruit and attract students at the graduate and undergraduate levels; and teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The assistant professor is expected to engage in teaching chemistry courses at the graduate and undergraduate level, mentor graduate and undergraduate students in research, recruit at both the graduate and undergraduate levels, pursue and establish an externally funded research program, and engage in shared governance of the university and department through service. The department will begin reviewing applicants beginning December 7, 2026. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in chemistry or closely related discipline. Post-doctoral experience is highly desirable. Special Conditions: Desired candidates will have a proposed research program that strengthens existing research activities in the department or addresses national and state research or workforce priorities in hypersonic materials, quantum computing, artificial intelligence, analytical chemistry or chemical education. See our website at http://www.memphis.edu/chem for additional information about on-going research. Visa Sponsorship Conditions: Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to sponsor or assume sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. Special Instructions to Candidates: You will need to submit a curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy and interest, description of proposed research with details of equipment needs, undergraduate and graduate transcripts, and three professional references with email contact information. Applicant Required Documents: Curriculum Vitae. Teaching Philosophy. Research Philosophy with startup budget. References List: Candidates should arrange to have three letters of recommendation uploaded. Unofficial Transcript – graduate and undergraduate. The successful candidate will be expected to: establish a vigorous, innovative, externally funded research program; recruit and attract students at the graduate and undergraduate levels; and teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/760": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/07/2027 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Assistant Professor, Computer Science The Department of Computer Science at the University of Memphis is seeking candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning Fall 2027. Qualified candidates in all areas of computer science are invited, while candidates with core expertise in AI/ML, including computer vision, deep fakes, LLMs, robotics, AI-assisted software engineering, and cybersecurity, as well as quantum computing are particularly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates are expected to develop externally sponsored research programs, lead or participate in collaborative research projects within Computer Science and beyond, teach both undergraduate and graduate courses, and provide academic advising to students at all levels. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science, or a related discipline, and be committed to excellence in both research and teaching for a diverse student body. Salary is highly competitive and dependent upon qualifications. We seek candidates who will contribute to a welcoming and supportive academic environment and encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of experiences and perspectives. The Department of Computer Science (www.memphis.edu/cs) offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs as well as graduate certificates in Data Science and Cybersecurity & Information Assurance. The Department has been ranked 55th among CS departments with federally funded research. The Department regularly engages in large federally funded collaborations across the nation. For example, CS faculty lead the NIH-funded mDOT National Research Center for Wearable AI and the Center for Information Assurance (CfIA). In addition, CS faculty work closely with multidisciplinary centers at the University such as the Institute for Intelligent Systems (IIS). The University of Memphis is a top-tier research university with a Carnegie R1 designation. The CS Department is housed in the College of Arts and Sciences (CAS), which offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing research, teaching, and service responsibilities in accordance with the College workload policy. Please refer to the CAS Teaching/Workload Guidelines for additional information. Known as America’s Number 1 logistics hub, the City of Memphis has been ranked as one of the “World’s Greatest Places” by TIME, as America’s 4th best city for jobs by Glassdoor, and 4th in “Best Cost of Living”. The Memphis metropolitan area has a population of 1.3 million. It boasts a vibrant culture and has a pleasant climate with an average temperature of 63 degrees. Screening of applications will begin on November 30, 2026, and will continue until the search is concluded. To apply, please visit https://workforum.memphis.edu/. Include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching and research statements, and three letters of recommendation. Inquiries can be directed to Scott Fleming (sdflming@memphis.edu). A background check will be required for employment. The University of Memphis is an Equal Opportunity University. Successful candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the start date of employment. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching, evidence of scholarly productivity, and the potential to establish and maintain an externally funded research program. Documented evidence of sustained ability in instruction, service, and research. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Provide high quality instruction, pursue excellence in research and scholarship, and contribute to meaningful service to the University. Contribute to the missions of teaching, research/scholarship, and public service."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/776": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/01/2027 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Department of Political Science at The University of Memphis invites applications for a Tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of public law, contributing to our growing pre-law program. The position will start in fall 2027 pending the availability of funds. We seek applications from candidates in any subfield who can contribute to our Pre-Law concentration. Preference will be given for candidates who focus on public law, and we have greater departmental needs in comparative politics or political theory. The successful candidate must show promise of excellence in research and teaching. The Department of Political Science at The University of Memphis invites applications for a Tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of public law, contributing to our growing pre-law program. The position will start in fall 2027 pending the availability of funds. We seek applications from candidates in any subfield who can contribute to our Pre-Law concentration. Preference will be given for candidates who focus on public law, and we have greater departmental needs in comparative politics or political theory. The successful candidate must show promise of excellence in research and teaching. The University of Memphis is an R1 institution. The College of Arts and Sciences offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing research, teaching, and service responsibilities in accordance with college workload policy. Please refer to the CAS Teaching/Workload Guidelines for additional information. Ph.D. in Political Science by August 16, 2027. Preferred Qualifications: Ability to teach courses such as Intro to Politics and Law, Intro to Law and Jurisprudence, Constitutional Law, and others in our Pre-Law curriculum. Ability to teach a graduate-level service course (i.e., Scope and Methods of Political Science, Political Statistics, or a survey Political Theory seminar). The successful candidate will be expected to: Teach undergraduate courses in the area of Public Law, including but not limited to Introduction to Politics and Law, Introduction to Law and Jurisprudence, Constitutional Law, and related offerings within the department's Pre-Law curriculum. Teach and support graduate-level service courses, such as Scope and Methods of Political Science, Political Statistics, or a graduate survey seminar in Political Theory. Maintain an active program of scholarly research and contribute to the advancement of knowledge in the field of Public Law. Contribute to the department's Public Law area and support the continued growth and development of its Pre-Law program through teaching, advising, curriculum development, student mentorship, and service activities. Participate actively in departmental, college, and university service. Workload Expectations: The College of Arts and Sciences offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing teaching, research, and service responsibilities in accordance with college workload policies. Faculty workload assignments are determined consistent with the College's teaching and workload guidelines and the needs of the department. Please submit a cover letter detailing current research and teaching experience, a curriculum vitae, a graduate transcript, evidence of teaching potential, and three professional references. For all other inquiries please contact: Assistant Professor Search Committee, Department of Political Science, The University of Memphis, TN 38152 (sastanly@memphis.edu). Review of applications will begin on January 5, 2027. The University of Memphis is an R1 institution. The College of Arts and Sciences offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing research, teaching, and service responsibilities in accordance with college workload policy. Please refer to the CAS Teaching/Workload Guidelines for additional information."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/743": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Assistant Professor, Public History Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Public History who may specialize in any practice of public history, any time period or geographic area who will develop connections with Memphis-area institutions. Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Public History who may specialize in any practice of public history, including but not limited to archival work, cultural resources management, digital history, film and media production, historic preservation, local history, museum exhibition and curation, oral history, and site interpretation. We welcome applications from historians of any time period or geographic area. We seek candidates who will develop connections with Memphis-area institutions. The process is online at https://workforum.memphis.edu. Choose \"Faculty,\" then \"Assistant Professor of Public History.\" All applicants will be expected to have at hand, ready for immediate upload: Letter of application Writing sample or detailed description of a public history project, accompanied by any relevant documentation Academic transcript Statement of teaching philosophy and sample syllabus Contact information (including email addresses) for three professional references Initial review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and may continue until the position is filled. Please contact search committee chair Susan Eva O'Donovan with any questions at odonovan@memphis.edu For more information about the Department of History, see: https://www.memphis.edu/history A Ph.D. in History is required upon appointment. Evidence of potential ability in instruction, service, and research. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Applicants must be authorized to work in the U. S. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. Please contact the Department of Human Resources in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. The University of Memphis is a Carnegie R 1 institution. Candidates are to provide high-quality instruction, pursue excellence in research/scholarship/creative activity, and contribute to meaningful service to the university and contribute to the missions of teaching, research / scholarship / creative activity, and public service. Teaching responsibilities include one survey-level course per semester that satisfies the university's General Education requirements in History and will offer also upper-level courses and graduate seminars that train students in the practices of public history."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/750": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of employment visas at this time. The Department of English at the University of Memphis invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in our Writing, Rhetoric, and Technical Communication graduate and undergraduate programs to begin Fall 2027. Candidates with expertise in areas such as engaged or public scholarship, grant writing, research methods, medical rhetoric, or technical editing are especially encouraged to apply. As a program and department committed to progressive pedagogies and curricular innovation, we seek a colleague who shares our commitment to inspiring socially-engaged citizenship in the classroom and in the community. The Writing, Rhetoric, and Technical Communication (WRTC) concentration within the Department of English houses BA, MA, and PhD programs with courses in technical communication, digital media, editing, grant writing, rhetorical theory, and composition. We seek candidates to help build our focus in technical communication and to continue growing our online MA in Technical Communication program. New faculty will be expected to maintain a productive research agenda, advise graduate students, and teach undergraduate and graduate courses. The Writing, Rhetoric, and Technical Communication program within the Department of English is uniquely positioned for community engagement and industry partnerships given that the University is located in a residential neighborhood of Memphis, a city with a rich history and vibrant culture. Memphis has a large medical hub (e.g., St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital, University of Tennessee’s College of Medicine, Southern College of Optometry) and is corporate headquarters for several major corporations (e.g., FedEx, International Paper, AutoZone). The University of Memphis is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. The Department of English at the University of Memphis invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in our Writing, Rhetoric, and Technical Communication graduate and undergraduate programs to begin Fall 2027. Candidates with expertise in areas such as engaged or public scholarship, grant writing, research methods, medical rhetoric, or technical editing are especially encouraged to apply. To Apply, candidates should submit the following materials: Cover letter Current CV Teaching Philosophy Writing Sample (15-20 pages) Transcripts Reference list Deadline: Review of applications will begin on November 6, 2026, and continue until position is filled. PhD in Technical Communication, Rhetoric and Composition, Writing Studies, or a related field. PhD must be in hand or secured by May 2027. A focused research agenda that includes publications or potential for publication in technical communication or related rhetorical fields Evidence of excellent in-person and online teaching in technical communication and writing studies A commitment to serving the needs of a diverse student population A collegial attitude for department and university service We seek candidates to help build our focus in technical communication and to continue growing our online MA in Technical Communication program. New faculty will be expected to maintain a productive research agenda, advise graduate students, and teach undergraduate and graduate courses. The University of Memphis is an R1 institution. The College of Arts and Sciences offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing research, teaching, and service responsibilities in accordance with College workload policy. Please refer to the CAS Teaching/Workload Guidelines for additional information."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/755": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/01/2027 Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The University of Memphis invites applications for an assistant professor tenure-track faculty position in Hypersonic Materials and Extreme Environments in the Department of Physics and Materials Science. The successful candidate will also hold the designation of the Memphis Institute for National Defense Sciences (MINDS) Distinguished Fellow in Hypersonic Materials and Extreme Environments. Salary will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. The University of Memphis invites applications for an assistant professor tenure-track faculty position in Hypersonic Materials and Extreme Environments in the Department of Physics and Materials Science. The successful candidate will also hold the designation of the Memphis Institute for National Defense Sciences (MINDS) Distinguished Fellow in Hypersonic Materials and Extreme Environments. Salary will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. The successful candidate will contribute scientific leadership to the University’s expanding hypersonics research portfolio within MINDS and will help develop research partnerships with the Naval Surface Warfare Center Carderock Division (NSWCCD) Memphis Detachment. The candidate will be expected to establish a nationally recognized, externally funded research program focused on high-temperature materials, extreme-environment materials science, and technologies supporting hypersonic and high-speed aerospace systems. Ph.D. in physics, materials science, materials engineering, mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, or a closely related field. Applicants must be U.S. citizens in order to obtain appropriate security clearance. Responsibilities will also include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in physics and materials science, mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, and participating in service activities within the Department, College, University, and broader professional community. The position will include a leadership role in the development and scientific direction of the MINDS Thermal Evaluation Readiness and Materials Analysis Laboratory (THERMAL), an emerging capability for flight-representative testing and characterization of advanced materials for Department of Defense applications. The University of Memphis is an R1 institution. The College of Arts and Sciences offers competitive faculty workloads that recognize demonstrated research productivity while balancing research, teaching, and service responsibilities in accordance with College workload policy. Please refer to the CAS Teaching/Workload Guidelines for additional information. THERMAL is being developed to enable rapid evaluation of thermal protection systems, refractory alloys, advanced ceramics, composites, radome materials, and other materials under combined thermal and aerodynamic loading representative of Mach 5–20 flight conditions. As the inaugural MINDS Hypersonics Distinguished Fellow, the successful candidate will help shape the University’s long-term strategy in hypersonics research, defense innovation, workforce development, and federal research laboratory collaboration."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/713": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United States Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Cognitive Psychology The University of Memphis Department of Psychology invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in Cognitive Psychology at the Assistant Professor level to begin August 2027, pending final budgetary approval. We seek an outstanding scholar whose research advances understanding of cognition and who complements existing departmental strengths in cognitive science, language, AI, learning, technology, and interdisciplinary research. The area of specialization is open, but preference will be given to candidates whose research meaningfully engages artificial intelligence, including generative AI, large language models, human-AI interaction, or related computational approaches to cognition and behavior. We are particularly interested in applicants with expertise in areas such as learning and memory, educational technology, human-computer interaction, cognitive modeling, artificial intelligence and cognition, computational approaches to behavior, or other emerging areas that connect cognitive science with real-world applications. Strong potential for external funding and interdisciplinary collaboration is especially valued. Successful candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Cognitive Psychology, Cognitive Science, or a closely related field by the start date of employment. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching, evidence of scholarly productivity, and the potential to establish and maintain an externally funded research program. The successful applicant will teach undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor graduate students, and contribute to the Department's collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment. We value the uniqueness of every individual and strive to ensure each person's success. Contributions from individuals with different backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives promote intellectual pluralism and enable us to achieve the excellence that we seek in learning, research, and engagement. This commitment makes our university a better place to work, learn, and innovate. In your application materials, please discuss your experiences and expertise that support these values and enrich our missions of teaching, research, and engagement. The Department of Psychology consists of more than 30 full-time faculty members, approximately 110 graduate students, and over 800 undergraduate majors. Graduate degree programs include a doctoral program in Experimental Psychology, an APA-accredited doctoral programs in Clinical Psychology and School Psychology, a master's degree program in General Psychology, and a NASP-accredited Ed.S. program in School Psychology. Faculty and students frequently collaborate across these programs and with researchers at the Institute for Intelligent Systems (IIS). The IIS is an interdisciplinary research center housed within the Edwards Research and Innovation Center that is dedicated to advancing knowledge and capabilities of intelligent systems, including psychological, biological, and artificial systems. The successful candidate will have opportunities to engage with internationally recognized interdisciplinary research teams spanning cognitive science, artificial intelligence, data science, education, engineering, and health. As a State of Tennessee Center of Excellence, the Psychology Department provides competitive salaries and strong support for faculty research and professional development. Faculty have a strong record of securing external funding from federal agencies including NSF, NIH, IES, DoD, and ONR. The University of Memphis is recognized as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and offers a highly collaborative environment for innovative and translational research. To apply, candidates must upload: A curriculum vitae, A cover letter describing research and teaching interests, Contact information for three professional reference providers, and Three selected publications. Review of applications will begin October 11, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants interested in this position should direct inquiries to search committee chair, Dr. Phil Pavlik, at ppavlik@memphis.edu. Successful candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Cognitive Psychology, Cognitive Science, or a closely related field by the start date of employment. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching, evidence of scholarly productivity, and the potential to establish and maintain an externally funded research program. Documented evidence of sustained ability in instruction, service, and research. Served at least five years at the rank of associate professor. Exceptions to this minimum rank qualification can be approved by the provost. Documented evidence of sustained high-quality professional productivity and national recognition in the academic discipline. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching, evidence of scholarly productivity, and the potential to establish and maintain an externally funded research program. The successful applicant will teach undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor graduate students, and contribute to the Department's collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment. Provide high-quality instruction, pursue excellence in research/scholarship/creative activity, and contribute to meaningful service to the university. Contribute to the missions of teaching, research / scholarship / creative activity, and public service."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/696": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Department of Management in the Fogelman College of Business & Economics actively seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor with a research specialization in Organizational Behavior (OB) and/or Human Resources Management (HRM), to begin in the fall semester of 2027, pending available funding. The Department of Management in the Fogelman College of Business & Economics actively seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor with a research specialization in Organizational Behavior (OB) and/or Human Resources Management (HRM), to begin in the fall semester of 2027, pending available funding. This is a 9-month appointment and offers competitive compensation, research support, and an attractive benefits program. THE COLLEGE The Fogelman College of Business and Economics has become a leading business school in the Southeast. Degree programs offered include the BBA, MBA, MA and MS, and PhD. The academic programs of the College are fully accredited by AACSB International. The College faculty of approximately 90 serves over 3,000 students, with approximately 2,300 undergraduates, 650 master's students, and 50 doctoral students. The Department of Management offers undergraduate degrees in both Management and Human Resource Management. Our highly-qualified faculty are recognized for their scholarship, publications, and editorships at leading academic journals. Candidates must have a Ph.D. with a concentration in management or a closely related discipline and show evidence of a strong research program targeting leading management journals (e.g., Academy of Management Journal, Journal of Management, Journal of Applied Psychology, Personnel Psychology, Journal of Management Studies, Journal of Organizational Behavior, Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes, Journal of Business and Psychology). Specific research interests within OB or HRM are open. Candidates must demonstrate a commitment to effective university teaching, having expertise or evident potential to provide quality teaching at both undergraduate and graduate levels, in both face-to-face and online formats. Successful candidates should demonstrate ability to contribute to doctoral education and mentoring of doctoral students. Research or teaching experience incorporating AI is welcome, but not necessary. Successful candidates must demonstrate a commitment to providing rank-appropriate service at the program, department, and college levels. Applications should include A cover letter outlining qualifications and interests A current curriculum vitae A research statement A teaching statement Two writing samples that demonstrate thought leadership Names and contact information for three professional references Maintaining a stream of high-quality research, effectively teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, mentoring doctoral students, participating in departmental activities, and fulfilling professional and university service responsibilities. The Department of Management is an academically diverse, research-oriented group with 15 full-time faculty members. Academic specializations include: HR/OB, D&I, entrepreneurship, and strategic management. The Department offers degree programs at all levels, including an active doctoral program, and is fully accredited by AACSB International. For more information, see: http://www.memphis.edu/management"
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/533": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Associate Professor Description POSITION The College of Education and the Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research seeks a full-time, 9-month, tenure- track faculty member for the Counseling Psychology program, beginning August 17, 2026. (Code 10535) The Counseling Psychology program is APA-accredited and emphasizes the development of professional competencies, a balanced scientist-practitioner training model, and the preparation of graduates for a wide variety of clinical and academic settings. The Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research and the College of Education are not currently offering any sponsorships for this position. SETTING The University of Memphis, an independently governed institution, is recognized by the Carnegie Foundation as one of two public research universities and the only community engaged public university in Tennessee. Boasting 12 colleges and professional schools, the University serves a diverse population of 22,000 students; 5,000 of whom are enrolled in graduate programs. Strongly committed to its urban mission and outreach programs, the University of Memphis recognizes the important role played by faculty and administrators in fulfilling a comprehensive mission of research, education, and community outreach. The Department of Counseling, Educational Psychology, and Research (CEPR) offers five graduate certificates, five master's degrees, and three doctoral degrees in its three principal areas. The department’s programs are accredited by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (see www.memphis.edu/cepr) and the American Psychological Association. The College of Education, with a faculty of over 75 full-time faculty positions, accredited by APA, CACREP, and CAEP is a leader in educator preparation. The College is dedicated to fairness and respect for the dignity of each person and seeks to prepare students for careers as administrators, counselors, educators, leaders, practitioners, researchers, scholars, and teachers. For additional information please see the websites of the university (http://www.memphis.edu), the college (http://www.memphis.edu/coe/), and the department (http:www.memphis.edu/cepr). SALARY & BEGINNING DATE Salary will be competitive and commensurate with qualifications. The University offers an excellent benefits package including healthcare and retirement. The position (code 10535) is a nine-month tenure track appointment with the possibility of summer teaching. The anticipated date of appointment is August 17, 2026. APPLICATION Review of applications will begin on October 20, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications are to be submitted electronically at https://workforum.memphis.edu. Applicants are required to upload (1) a letter of application, (2) a statement of scholarship (research and grants, if applicable), (3) a statement of teaching experience and philosophy, (4) a curriculum vitae, and (5) names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three references. Applicants will receive a confirmation number when all application materials are submitted. Address inquiries and nominations to Dr. Sara Bridges at sbridges@memphis.edu. Documented evidence of ability in instruction, service, and research. Served six years at the rank of assistant professor. Exceptions to this minimum rank qualification can be approved by the provost. Documented evidence of high-quality professional productivity which is leading to national recognition in the academic discipline. Professional comportment consistent with the Faculty Code of Conduct Qualifications • Earned doctoral degree (obtained by August 1st, 2026) in Counseling Psychology from an APA- accredited program • Evidence of an emerging (Assistant) or established (Associate) research agenda and publication record • Psychology licensure or license eligibility in the State of Tennessee Desired • Evidence of funded grant awards or participation in funded projects • Experience teaching graduate courses (in-person, online or hybrid format) • Interest or experience in teaching cognitive and personality assessment • Experience providing clinical supervision, including supervision of cognitive and personality assessment • Interest in serving in the Training Director role (Associate) Provide high quality instruction, pursue excellence in research/scholarship/creative activity, and contribute to meaningful service to the university. Contribute to the missions of teaching, research / scholarship / creative activity, and public service."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/831": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (EECE) at the University of Memphis invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning August 2027. We seek a scholar who will develop an externally funded research program addressing significant societal and technological challenges through electrical and computer engineering. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor M.S. and Ph.D. students, contribute to curriculum development, and participate in departmental, college, and University service. University, College, and Department The University of Memphis is a Carnegie Research 1 public university serving 19,652 students in Fall 2025 and employing more than 1,000 full-time faculty. The Herff College of Engineering supports interdisciplinary research and education that address regional, national, and global needs. EECE includes 12 tenured and tenure-track faculty and approximately 348 students. The Department offers two accredited undergraduate programs with a combined enrollment of approximately 312 students, an M.S. program with approximately 12 students, and Electrical and Computer Engineering concentrations within the Ph.D. in Engineering with approximately 24 students. Its principal research strengths are Electrical Systems, Biomedical Systems, and Intelligent Information Systems. Faculty collaborate across the University and with external partners in areas that include biomedical imaging and sensing, remote sensing for defense, agriculture, and environmental applications, autonomous navigation, robotics, cybersecurity, healthcare, transportation, and resilient infrastructure. Additional information is available from the Herff College of Engineering and the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering . Research and Teaching Focus The Department seeks a scholar whose research applies electrical and computer engineering to major societal and technological challenges. Priority will be given to candidates whose work strengthens one or more of the following interconnected areas: Intelligent sensing and healthcare technologies, including biomedical sensors, imaging, wearable and embedded systems, assistive technologies, and AI-enabled health monitoring. Secure and trustworthy computing, including cybersecurity, privacy, hardware security, trustworthy AI, and protection of cyber-physical systems and critical infrastructure. Sustainable energy and electrification, including power electronics, smart grids and microgrids, renewable-energy integration, energy storage, electric transportation, and wide-bandgap semiconductor systems. Next-generation computing, communications, and autonomous systems, including quantum information technologies, edge and neuromorphic computing, advanced wireless systems, robotics, autonomous navigation, and remote sensing. Strong candidates will demonstrate how their research addresses a significant societal or technological challenge, complements existing departmental strengths, supports interdisciplinary collaboration, and can attract sustained external funding. Why Memphis and the Herff College of Engineering Memphis is an affordable, culturally rich metropolitan area with strengths in logistics and transportation, healthcare and medical devices, advanced manufacturing, information technology, and related industries. These sectors create opportunities for applied research, technology translation, student engagement, and external partnership. The Herff College of Engineering offers a collaborative environment for research and education in intelligent systems, healthcare technologies, resilience, sustainability, and infrastructure. The University of Memphis is recognized by the Carnegie Classification as Research 1: Very High Spending and Doctorate Production. Application Instructions Applicants must submit materials through the University of Memphis faculty employment portal at https://workforum.memphis.edu/ and select Full-Time Faculty. A complete application must include: A cover letter describing the applicant's interest in the position and alignment with the Department. A research statement describing past contributions and a future agenda, including how the proposed work addresses significant societal or technological challenges and complements EECE strengths. A statement of teaching philosophy and interests. A current curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and may continue until the position is filled. Required Qualifications An earned doctorate in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related field by the appointment date. Evidence of the potential to establish and sustain a strong, independent, externally funded research program. A demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. Preferred Qualifications Research expertise in one or more of the priority areas identified above. A record of, or clear potential for, interdisciplinary and collaborative research. Potential to develop partnerships with industry, healthcare, government, or research organizations. Responsibilities Develop and sustain an innovative, externally funded research program aligned with departmental and college priorities. Teach core and advanced Electrical and Computer Engineering courses and develop courses in the candidate's areas of expertise. Mentor undergraduate and graduate students and support their professional development. Contribute to curriculum development, shared governance, and service to the Department, College, University, and profession. Collaborate across the College and University, and with external partners on high-impact research and educational initiatives."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/832": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Teaching, Manufacturing and Operations",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Polytechnic@UofM at the University of Memphis invites applications for an Assistant/Associate Professor of Teaching in Manufacturing and Operations. This 12-month teaching position adds capacity in the manufacturing and operations teaching areas that run through nearly every program the Polytechnic offers, at both the undergraduate and graduate level. In addition, the Polytechnic is committed to integrating AI into its programs, and this program will be integral to those efforts. Assistant/Associate Professor of Teaching, Manufacturing and Operations The Polytechnic@UofM, University of Memphis Position Summary The Polytechnic@UofM at the University of Memphis invites applications for an Assistant/Associate Professor of Teaching in Manufacturing and Operations. This 12-month teaching position adds capacity in the manufacturing and operations teaching areas that run through nearly every program the Polytechnic offers, at both the undergraduate and graduate level. In addition, the Polytechnic is committed to integrating AI into its programs, and this program will be integral to those efforts. The successful candidate will teach how work is designed and measured, how quality is built into a process, how production and distribution are planned and controlled, and how facilities are laid out to make all of that work. These courses serve students in the Bachelor of Science in Engineering Technology and in the Bachelor of Applied Science (BAS) programs in Manufacturing Supervision, Construction Leadership, Information Technology, and Applied Artificial Intelligence, as well as graduate students in the Master of Science in Engineering Technology and the manufacturing concentration of the Master of Science in Engineering Management. Memphis is one of the most concentrated distribution and logistics economies in the United States, and the region's employers hire directly into the operations, quality, and facilities roles this coursework prepares students for. The position is a good fit for someone who wants their teaching to sit close to working plants and distribution centers, and who is interested in helping the department extend into materials handling, automation, and production process design. Application Process Applicants must apply online via the University of Memphis WorkforUM site. Required application materials include: A detailed letter of application addressing qualifications and teaching philosophy. A current curriculum vitae. Contact information for three professional or academic references. Screening of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Required Qualifications A Master's degree in engineering technology, operations or supply chain management, or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated record of excellent teaching at the university level or substantial professional experience in manufacturing, distribution, or operations. Experience with and commitment to online and blended learning delivery, as a growing share of the department's coursework is offered virtually. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Preferred Qualifications A terminal degree (Ph.D. or equivalent) in a relevant field. Professional certification such as ASQ Certified Quality Engineer or Certified Six Sigma Black Belt, SME Certified Manufacturing Engineer or Technologist, ASCM/APICS CPIM or CSCP, or PMP. Teaching or practice experience in lean methods and manufacturing strategy. Experience with materials handling systems, warehouse and distribution operations, or facility layout and simulation software. Experience with automation, robotics, or programmable control in a production setting. Working knowledge of ERP, MRP, or production scheduling systems. Experience applying artificial intelligence and data analysis tools to manufacturing and operations problems. Proven experience in curriculum development for online and in-person delivery. Experience supporting an ABET-accredited engineering technology program. Evidence of a commitment to fostering a diverse and inclusive learning environment. Duties and Responsibilities Teaching and instruction: Deliver a full teaching load of four courses in fall and four in spring, with summer teaching based on departmental needs, across undergraduate and graduate sections. Core teaching areas include work design and improvement, quality improvement and statistical quality methods, production and operations planning and control, and facility design and layout. Responsibilities include developing and updating course materials and syllabi, leading class and laboratory sessions, and assessing student work through assignments, projects, and examinations. Graduate project supervision: Direct graduate student projects and internships, guide students through project definition and execution, and serve on and chair graduate project committees. Curriculum development: Participate in the ongoing development and revision of the curriculum. The department is actively building out coursework in materials handling and automated systems, and in production processes and process planning. The successful candidate will have real latitude to shape and launch this coursework. Online course development: Develop and deliver online and blended sections. The department's graduate and BAS programs are moving toward fully online, and the manufacturing and operations courses are a central part of that shift. Student advising and mentorship: Serve as an academic advisor to undergraduate and graduate students, guiding them on academic pathways and careers in manufacturing, quality, and operations. Program and departmental service: Contribute to program assessment and to the continuous-improvement and reporting work that supports ABET accreditation of the Engineering Technology program, and participate in committees and other service supporting the mission of the Polytechnic@UofM. Industry engagement: Maintain working relationships with regional manufacturers, distributors, and logistics employers; support student internships, projects, and placement; and bring current industry practice into the classroom. Professional engagement: Stay current with developments in manufacturing and operations practice, including the growing role of data and artificial intelligence in operations, and engage in activities that foster professional growth."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/833": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Health Sciences and Lifestyle Medicine",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Assistant Professor of Health Sciences and Lifestyle Medicine within the College of Health and Human Sciences (CHHS) is a full-time, tenure-track, 9-month faculty position. The anticipated start date of employment is August 16, 2027. An earned doctoral degree in Health Promotion, Lifestyle Medicine, or a closely related field is required. Post doctoral training is required. Candidates must demonstrate evidence of, or strong potential for, developing a focused and sustainable research agenda in lifestyle medicine, chronic disease prevention, health behavior change, or a closely related area, as evidenced by a publication record and/or externally funded grants or contracts. Strong oral, written, and interpersonal communication skills are required. University level teaching experience is preferred. Candidate must meet eligibility requirements to begin and continue working in the United States at the time of appointment. The review of completed applications will begin upon receipt and continue until the position is filled. Only applications submitted electronically at WorkforUM Careers will be considered. Applicants are required to upload (1) a cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) three professional references (name and contact information) from colleagues who can address the applicant’s professional preparation for this position. Applicants should receive a confirmation number when all application materials are received. For questions about this position please contact Michelle Stockton, Search Committee Chair, at mstocktn@memphis.edu. Develop and grow an externally funded research program in the area of lifestyle medicine. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in epidemiology and public health, as well as courses related to lifestyle medicine and other areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and program needs. Courses will be delivered in traditional (in-person), laboratory, hybrid, and online formats. Serve as a mentor to undergraduate and graduate students. Engage in service activities at the academic unit, college, university, and professional levels. The College of Health and Human Sciences (https://www.memphis.edu/healthsciences/) offers the B.S. degree in Health Studies with concentrations in Health Sciences; Dietetics; Human Nutrition; Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences; and Healthcare Leadership; the B.S. in Sport and Entertainment Management; the B.A degree in Hospitality and Resort Management (through the Kemmons Wilson School of Hospitality and Resort Management); and the B.S.Ed. degree in Physical Education Teacher Education. The college also offers the M.S. degree in Health Studies with concentrations in Exercise Nutrition; Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences; Lifestyle Medicine; and Physical Education Teacher Education. A second M.S. degree is offered in Sport Commerce, with a third M.S. degree offered in Nutrition with concentrations in Integrated Nutrition; Environmental Nutrition; and Nutrition Science. The college also offers PhD programs in Applied Biomechanics and Applied Physiology and Nutrition, as well as the Doctor of Physical Therapy. Multiple laboratories are available within the College, with an extensive equipment inventory. Additionally, a major research modernization project is planned for 2027. There are approximately 1900 undergraduate and graduate majors within CHHS. The University of Memphis is recognized by the Carnegie Foundation as a Research 1 Institution, one of only three in the state of TN. It has a diverse population of approximately 20,000 students including approximately 4,000 graduate students."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/836": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States The Assistant Professor of Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences within the College of Health and Human Sciences (CHHS) is a full-time, tenure-track, 9-month faculty position. The anticipated start date of employment is August 16, 2027. An earned doctoral degree in Exercise Science, Physiology, or a closely related field is required. Post-doctoral training is required. Documented evidence of a focused line of research inquiry in the area of cardiovascular physiology or a closely related area is required, as evidenced by a publication record and/or externally funded grants or contracts. Strong oral, written, and interpersonal communication skills are required. University level teaching experience is preferred. Candidate must meet eligibility requirements to begin and continue working in the United States at the time of appointment. The review of completed applications will begin upon receipt and continue until the position is filled. Only applications submitted electronically at WorkforUM Careers will be considered. Applicants are required to upload (1) a cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position, (2) curriculum vitae, and (3) three professional references (name and contact information) from colleagues who can address the applicant’s professional preparation for this position. Applicants should receive a confirmation number when all application materials are received. For questions about this position please contact Melissa Puppa, PhD, Search Committee Chair, at mpuppa@memphis.edu. Develop and grow an externally funded research program in the area of cardiovascular physiology, preferably in the area of exercise science. Research may be done at the cell culture, pre-clinical, or clinical level. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in cardiovascular exercise physiology and related areas. Courses may be delivered in traditional (in-person), laboratory, hybrid, and online formats. Serve as a mentor to undergraduate and graduate students. Engage in service activities at the academic unit, college, university, and professional levels. The College of Health and Human Sciences (https://www.memphis.edu/healthsciences/) offers the B.S. degree in Health Studies with concentrations in Health Sciences; Dietetics; Human Nutrition; Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences; and Healthcare Leadership; the B.S. in Sport and Entertainment Management; the B.A degree in Hospitality and Resort Management (through the Kemmons Wilson School of Hospitality and Resort Management); and the B.S.Ed. degree in Physical Education Teacher Education. The college also offers the M.S. degree in Health Studies with concentrations in Exercise Nutrition; Exercise, Sport & Movement Sciences; Lifestyle Medicine; and Physical Education Teacher Education. A second M.S. degree is offered in Sport Commerce, with a third M.S. degree offered in Nutrition with concentrations in Integrated Nutrition; Environmental Nutrition; and Nutrition Science. The college also offers PhD programs in Applied Biomechanics and Applied Physiology and Nutrition, as well as the Doctor of Physical Therapy. Multiple laboratories are available within the College, with an extensive equipment inventory. Additionally, a major research modernization project is planned for 2027. There are approximately 1900 undergraduate and graduate majors within CHHS. The University of Memphis is recognized by the Carnegie Foundation as a Research 1 Institution, one of only three in the state of TN. It has a diverse population of approximately 20,000 students including approximately 4,000 graduate students."
    },
    "https://ibqajb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/806": {
      "title": "Clinical Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Memphis, TN, United States Clinical Postdoctoral Fellow responsible for overseeing treatment services at The Gambling Clinic, including clinical supervision, documentation review, quality assurance, post-treatment program development, and professional training activities. Clinical Postdoctoral Fellow with responsibilities primarily dedicated to the treatment workflow at The Gambling Clinic. Primary duties include clinical supervision of clinical staff and peer recovery specialists, oversight of clinic documentation, and the development and implementation of post-treatment evaluations and services. A portion of the position is also dedicated to various professional development opportunities. Ph.D in Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Counseling, or related field. Familiarity with gambling addiction, research and treatment REQUIRED. Applicants must be authorized to work in the U.S. We are unable to sponsor or take over sponsorship of an employment visa at this time. FTE Job Responsibility Supervision (as directed by MKG) Provide 1 on 1 supervision to trainees Lead one supervision team Provide didactics as required Review clinical notes Supervision of Peer Recovery Specialist Collaborate with Client Retention Manager Client flow challenges Completion of follow up assessment Development of Post-treatment supports Supervision with MKG Professional Development (including application for licensure) Other duties as assigned or negotiated"
    }
  },
